    "activities": [
      {
        "id": "68ff2798-5438-5aac-89c6-c489c009aeb8",
        "kind": "lesson",
        "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
        "title": "Meet the right triangle",
        "summary": "Legs, the hypotenuse, and the pattern Pythagoras is famous for.",
        "body": "## What makes a triangle “right”?\n\nA **right triangle** has one angle of exactly 90° — a square corner, like the corner of a page. The two sides that make the square corner are called the **legs**. The side opposite the square corner is the **hypotenuse**. It is always the longest side.\n\n![A right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rtriangle.svg?width=800 \"A right triangle: legs a and b meet at the square corner; c is the hypotenuse. Image: Gustavb, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\n> [!TIP]\n> To find the hypotenuse, put your finger on the square corner. The side your finger is **not** touching is the hypotenuse.\n\n## The big idea\n\nMore than 2,500 years ago, mathematicians noticed something remarkable. If you draw a square on each side of a right triangle, the two smaller squares together have exactly the same area as the big square on the hypotenuse.\n\n![Squares drawn on the three sides of a right triangle](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pythagorean.svg?width=800 \"The areas of the squares on the two legs add up to the area of the square on the hypotenuse. Image: Wapcaplet, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\nWritten as a rule, with legs *a* and *b* and hypotenuse *c*:\n\n**a² + b² = c²**\n\nThis is the **Pythagorean theorem**, named after the Greek thinker Pythagoras — although people in Babylon, India and China knew the pattern too.\n\n## Watch: the theorem in action\n\n![The Pythagorean theorem intro](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AA6RfgP-AHU)\n\n*Video: “The Pythagorean theorem intro” by Khan Academy, on YouTube.*\n\n## Check yourself\n\n- Which side of a right triangle is always the longest?\n- Does the theorem work for a triangle with no right angle? (No — only right triangles.)",
        "objectives": [
          "Identify the hypotenuse and the legs of a right triangle",
          "Explain what the Pythagorean theorem says"
        ],
        "difficulty": 1,
        "estimatedMinutes": 10,
        "maxAttempts": null,
        "passingScore": 0,
        "feedbackMode": "immediate",
