      {
        "id": "f53b1007-d439-5519-8c43-60844652cfe4",
        "kind": "lesson",
        "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
        "title": "Using the theorem: finding a missing side",
        "summary": "Worked examples, Pythagorean triples and real-life uses.",
        "body": "## Finding the hypotenuse\n\nIf you know both legs, square them, add, then take the square root.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** The legs are 6 cm and 8 cm.\n>\n> 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and √100 = **10 cm**.\n\n## Finding a missing leg\n\nIf you know the hypotenuse and one leg, **subtract** instead of adding: b² = c² − a².\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Example.** The hypotenuse is 13 m and one leg is 5 m.\n>\n> 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, and √144 = **12 m**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A common slip is adding when you should subtract. Ask: “Am I looking for the longest side?” If yes, add. If not, subtract.\n\n## Pythagorean triples\n\nSome right triangles have whole-number sides. These sets are worth knowing — they save time:\n\n| Leg a | Leg b | Hypotenuse c | Check |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 3 | 4 | 5 | 9 + 16 = 25 |\n| 5 | 12 | 13 | 25 + 144 = 169 |\n| 8 | 15 | 17 | 64 + 225 = 289 |\n| 7 | 24 | 25 | 49 + 576 = 625 |\n\nMultiples work too: 6, 8, 10 is just 3, 4, 5 doubled.\n\n## Where you’ll meet it\n\n- **Ladders:** a 5 m ladder with its foot 3 m from a wall reaches 4 m up the wall.\n- **Screens:** a TV’s size is measured along its diagonal — the hypotenuse.\n- **Maps:** the straight-line distance between two points on a grid.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Always sketch the triangle and mark the square corner before you calculate.",
        "objectives": [
          "Find the hypotenuse using a² + b² = c²",
          "Find a missing leg of a right triangle"
        ],
