    "id": "a231cf67-02f8-5b35-8207-6eaf025ba871",
    "sample": "cells-photosynthesis",
    "subjectSlug": "science",
    "title": "Sample: Cells & Photosynthesis",
    "description": "A ready-made biology sample: what cells are, how plant and animal cells differ, and how plants make food from light. Two illustrated lessons with videos and a quiz. Edit anything — it’s your copy.",
    "joinCode": "SMPBIO1",
    "module": {
      "id": "751ddb66-16f1-543e-9ba7-c6e0c3a132f4",
      "title": "Unit 1: Living things",
      "description": "Cells, and how plants turn light into food."
    },
    "activities": [
      {
        "id": "15731a69-2a89-5b81-b301-f141c2399f98",
        "kind": "lesson",
        "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
        "title": "Cells: the building blocks of life",
        "summary": "The cell theory, organelles, and how plant and animal cells differ.",
        "body": "## The smallest unit of life\n\nEvery living thing — a tree, a mushroom, a whale, you — is made of **cells**. Some living things are a single cell. You are made of tens of trillions of them, working together.\n\nScientists sum this up in the **cell theory**:\n\n1. All living things are made of one or more cells.\n2. The cell is the basic unit of life.\n3. New cells come only from cells that already exist.\n\n## Inside an animal cell\n\n![Diagram of an animal cell and its parts](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Animal_cell_structure_en.svg?width=800 \"An animal cell and its organelles. Image: LadyofHats (Mariana Ruiz), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\nCells contain tiny parts called **organelles**, each with a job — like the departments of a factory.\n\n## Inside a plant cell\n\n![Diagram of a plant cell and its parts](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Plant_cell_structure-en.svg?width=800 \"A plant cell: notice the cell wall, chloroplasts and large vacuole. Image: LadyofHats (Mariana Ruiz), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\n## Plant and animal cells compared\n\n| Part | Its job | Animal cell | Plant cell |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Nucleus | Holds DNA and controls the cell | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Cell membrane | Controls what goes in and out | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Cytoplasm | Jelly where most reactions happen | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Mitochondria | Release energy from food (respiration) | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Ribosomes | Make proteins | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Cell wall | Strengthens and supports the cell | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Chloroplasts | Make food by photosynthesis | ✗ | ✓ |\n| Large permanent vacuole | Stores cell sap and keeps the cell firm | ✗ | ✓ |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Memory trick: plants have **three extras** — the cell **W**all, **C**hloroplasts and a big **V**acuole. “Walls, Chloroplasts, Vacuole.”\n\n## Watch: a tour of the cell\n\n![Introduction to Cells: The Grand Cell Tour](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IlzKri08kk)\n\n*Video: “Introduction to Cells: The Grand Cell Tour” by the Amoeba Sisters, on YouTube.*",
        "objectives": [
          "Describe what a cell is and state the cell theory",
          "Compare plant and animal cells"
        ],
        "difficulty": 1,
        "estimatedMinutes": 15,
        "maxAttempts": null,
        "passingScore": 0,
        "feedbackMode": "immediate",
        "allowSkills": false,
        "boss": null,
        "prerequisiteIds": [],
        "questions": []
      },
      {
        "id": "af13215e-1eed-544f-b5b9-130d09aac411",
        "kind": "lesson",
        "xpSource": "lesson_completion",
        "title": "Photosynthesis: how plants make food",
        "summary": "The ingredients, the products, and where it all happens.",
        "body": "## How do plants eat?\n\nPlants don’t eat food the way animals do. They **make** their own food — a sugar called **glucose** — using light. This process is called **photosynthesis** (“putting together with light”).\n\n![A close-up of a green leaf](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leaf_1_web.jpg?width=800 \"Leaves are the plant’s food factories. Image: Jon Sullivan, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\n## The ingredients and the products\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen**\n>\n> This needs **light** energy, captured by **chlorophyll**.\n\n- **Carbon dioxide** enters the leaf from the air through tiny holes called stomata.\n- **Water** travels up from the roots.\n- **Glucose** is used for energy and growth, or stored as starch.\n- **Oxygen** is released into the air — the oxygen we breathe.\n\n![Diagram of photosynthesis showing light, water, carbon dioxide, sugar and oxygen](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Photosynthesis_en.svg?width=800 \"Photosynthesis at a glance. Image: At09kg, Wattcle and Nefronus, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons\")\n\n## Where it happens\n\nPhotosynthesis happens inside **chloroplasts**, mostly in leaf cells. Chloroplasts contain **chlorophyll**, a green pigment that absorbs light energy (mainly red and blue light). Chlorophyll reflects green light — that’s why leaves look green.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Plants **respire** all the time, day and night, just like animals. In daylight, photosynthesis is usually faster than respiration, so a plant gives out more oxygen than it uses.\n\n## Watch: photosynthesis explained\n\n![Photosynthesis: Crash Course Biology #8](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sQK3Yr4Sc_k)\n\n*Video: “Photosynthesis: Crash Course Biology #8” by CrashCourse, on YouTube. It goes into more detail than you need — watch the first few minutes for the big picture.*",
        "objectives": [
          "Write the word equation for photosynthesis",
          "Explain the role of chloroplasts and chlorophyll"
        ],
